## 20. Troubleshooting Experience

Several real operational issues were encountered and investigated during
the implementation.

### Kubernetes CPU Scheduling

A new Navigation pod remained:

``` text
Pending
```

Node metrics showed relatively low actual CPU usage, but Kubernetes node
allocation showed approximately 95-96% of allocatable CPU already
reserved by workload requests.

This demonstrated an important Kubernetes scheduling concept:

> Kubernetes schedules pods using resource requests rather than current
> CPU utilization.

Useful diagnostic commands included:

``` bash
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp
kubectl top nodes
kubectl top pods --sort-by=cpu
kubectl describe nodes
```

### Delta Node-Pool Capacity

A temporary attempt to increase Delta from two to three nodes
encountered:

``` text
ZONE_RESOURCE_POOL_EXHAUSTED
```

The node pool entered an error state because the requested VM capacity
was unavailable in the selected zone.

Rather than rebuilding the lab in another zone, the desired node count
was returned to two.

The node pool recovered to:

``` text
status: RUNNING
```

This demonstrated the difference between Kubernetes scheduling capacity
and underlying Compute Engine zonal capacity.

### Gateway API Verification

The Gateway API configuration was not visible using the initially
attempted top-level output path.

The correct cluster field was:

``` text
networkConfig.gatewayApiConfig
```

### Fleet CLI Difference

The installed gcloud CLI did not accept:

``` text
--location=global
```

for the Fleet feature describe command.

The working command omitted that option.

### Deployment Annotation vs Live Specification

`kubectl get deployment -o yaml` contained an older configuration inside
the:

``` text
kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration
```

annotation.

The live image and environment variables were therefore verified
directly using JSONPath against:

``` text
.spec.template.spec.containers
```

This prevented an old annotation from being mistaken for the current
Deployment specification.

